@style(../styles/IDocDemo.style)
@title(Octo 14: the SAS procedures)

Octo can print a report the way SAS prints it. Each `proc...` function takes a table and prints the report; with an output argument it returns a table instead. The data is invented.


@section(PROC MEANS)

Summary statistics, overall or per class level; `"Stats"` picks which ones.

@octo
group = categorical(["c"; "c"; "c"; "c"; "t"; "t"; "t"; "t"]);
gain  = [2.1; 2.9; 3.8; 5.2; 3.0; 4.1; 5.3; 6.4];
T = table(group, gain);
procmeans(T, {"gain"}, "Class", "group")
R = procmeans(T, {"gain"}, "Stats", {"n", "mean", "median", "clm"})
@end

@section(PROC FREQ)

One-way counts with percentages, or a two-way table with chi-square tests.

@octo
color = categorical({"red"; "blue"; "red"; "red"; "blue"; "green"; "red"; "blue"});
size_ = categorical({"S"; "L"; "S"; "L"; "L"; "S"; "S"; "L"});
T = table(color, size_);
procfreq(T, "color")
procfreq(T, {"color", "size_"})
@end

@section(PROC CORR)

Pearson correlations with their p-values and the simple statistics.

@octo
dose = [1; 2; 3; 4; 1; 2; 3; 4];
gain = [2.1; 2.9; 3.8; 5.2; 3.0; 4.1; 5.3; 6.4];
before = [60; 62; 59; 64; 61; 63; 58; 65];
proccorr(table(dose, gain, before), {"dose", "gain", "before"})
@end

@section(PROC TTEST)

Two groups (pooled and Satterthwaite), one sample against `H0`, or paired.

@octo
group = categorical(["c"; "c"; "c"; "c"; "t"; "t"; "t"; "t"]);
gain  = [2.1; 2.9; 3.8; 5.2; 3.0; 4.1; 5.3; 6.4];
before = [60; 62; 59; 64; 61; 63; 58; 65];
after  = [62; 63; 62; 66; 64; 65; 61; 69];
T = table(group, gain, before, after);
procttest(T, "gain", "Class", "group")
procttest(T, "gain", "H0", 3)
procttest(T, {"after", "before"}, "Paired", true)
@end

@section(PROC ANOVA and PROC REG)

`procanova` treats each effect as a class variable; `procreg` is linear regression with SAS's report.

@octo
group = categorical(["c"; "c"; "c"; "c"; "t"; "t"; "t"; "t"]);
dose  = [1; 2; 3; 4; 1; 2; 3; 4];
gain  = [2.1; 2.9; 3.8; 5.2; 3.0; 4.1; 5.3; 6.4];
T = table(group, dose, gain);
procanova(T, "gain ~ group + dose")
procreg(T, "gain ~ dose")
@end
